1. Volume Management is the concept, while LVM (Logical Volume Manager) is one implementation of that concept in Linux.

2. What is Volume Management?

Volume Management means managing disk storage efficiently.

Instead of using disks directly, we create logical storage (volumes) that are easier to manage.

Its main purpose is to:

Combine multiple disks into one storage
Increase storage without reinstalling the OS
Manage disk space easily
Real-life Example

Imagine you have two water tanks.

Tank 1 = 100 Litres
Tank 2 = 100 Litres

Instead of using them separately, you connect them.

Now you have one large tank of 200 Litres.

3. Example without Volume Management

Suppose your server has:

Disk1 = 100 GB
Disk2 = 100 GB

The operating system sees them separately.

Disk1 (100 GB)

Disk2 (100 GB)

If Disk1 becomes full, you cannot directly use the free space from Disk2.

4. Example with Volume Management

Now use Volume Management.

Disk1 (100 GB)

Disk2 (100 GB)

↓

One Volume = 200 GB

Now the operating system sees only one storage of 200 GB.

This is easier to manage.

5. What is LVM?

LVM (Logical Volume Manager) is the Linux tool used for Volume Management.

6. How LVM Works

LVM has three main components.

Step 1: Physical Volume (PV)

A Physical Volume is an actual disk.

Example:

/dev/sdb

/dev/sdc

These are your real hard disks

Step 2: Volume Group (VG)

A Volume Group combines multiple Physical Volumes.

Example:

Disk1 = 100 GB

Disk2 = 100 GB

↓

Volume Group = 200 GB

Think of the Volume Group as one big storage pool.

Step 3: Logical Volume (LV)

From the Volume Group, we create Logical Volumes.

Example:

Volume Group = 200 GB

↓

LV1 = 80 GB

LV2 = 60 GB

LV3 = 60 GB

These Logical Volumes behave like normal partitions.

You can format them, mount them, and store files on them.

7. Lvm flow

Physical Disk

↓

Physical Volume (PV)

↓

Volume Group (VG)

↓

Logical Volume (LV)

↓

Filesystem (ext4/xfs)

↓

Mount Point (/data)

8. Real-Life Example

Suppose a company has:

Disk1 = 200 GB

Disk2 = 300 GB
Step 1

Create Physical Volumes.

PV1 = 200 GB

PV2 = 300 GB
Step 2

Combine them.

VG = 500 GB

Step 3

Create Logical Volumes.

HR = 100 GB

Finance = 150 GB

Backup = 250 GB

Every department gets its own storage.

9. What if HR needs more space?

Initially,

HR = 100 GB

Later HR stores many files.

Now it needs 50 GB more.

With LVM,

HR

100 GB

↓

150 GB

You can extend the Logical Volume without creating a new partition (provided free space is available in the Volume Group).

This is one of the biggest advantages of LVM.

10. Where does Solaris come into this?

Solaris also has Volume Management.

Instead of Linux LVM, Solaris traditionally used Solaris Volume Manager (SVM) and later ZFS for storage management.

11. LVM vs Solaris Volume Management

| Linux (LVM)          | Solaris Volume Management  |
| -------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Physical Volume (PV) | Physical Disk              |
| Volume Group (VG)    | Disk Set / Storage Pool    |
| Logical Volume (LV)  | Volume                     |
| ext4 / XFS           | ZFS / UFS                  |
| Uses LVM commands    | Uses Solaris storage tools |

